#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129519 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/60/603dc3142d8329fd9ff9fe8d09b131fe09497405f761918d59261fde314772c6.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi /2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129525 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(1,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+v \\\\ 2u-{v}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3{v}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-3{v}^{2})-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6u{v}^{2}-2 \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,2)} & =(-6u{v}^{2}-2)\\,{|}_{u=1\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-6(1)(2)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-26|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =26\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129526 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-{w}^{2} \\\\ w{e}^{u} \\\\ u-v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-{w}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u},\\,z(u,v,w)=u-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 & -2w \\\\ w{e}^{u} & 0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\begin{vmatrix}0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2w\\begin{vmatrix}w{e}^{u} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u(0+{e}^{u})-2w(-w{e}^{u}-0) \\\\  & =2u{e}^{u}+2{w}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u) & =0 \\\\ {w}^{2}+u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${w}^{2}+u=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129527 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(1,5)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}(uv) \\\\ u{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 2uv\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2uv-\\frac{1}{v}\\cdot {v}^{2} \\\\  & =2v-v \\\\  & =v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,5)} & =v\\,{|}_{u=1\\,v=5} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|5|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =5\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129561 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-9$ and a maximum value of $1$." :question/worked-solution "The midline lies halfway between the minimum and maximum: $$y=\\frac{-9+1}{2}=-4.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129562 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed cosine function with a minimum value of $-5$ and a maximum value of $9.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-5+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129626 :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }2r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}[{r}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129627 :question/problem "![](images/bc/bccbe3973fa63908f5e264f4566e1207c5019dc1de9c36ef42481428527aef3b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129628 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}y\\ge 0\\}$, then $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)x\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The circle becomes $r=2\\cos\\theta$; restricting to $y\\ge0$ gives $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$. Since $x\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\,dA=r^3\\cos\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, use\n$$\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^{2\\cos\\theta}r^3\\cos\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129629 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2u\\operatorname{cos}v \\\\ 3u\\operatorname{sin}v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$ and $y(u,v)=3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-(-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =6u({cos}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129631 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{π/3}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r^{2}\\sin θdrdθ\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "Separate the two factors:\n$$\\int_{\\pi/3}^{\\pi/2}\\int_1^2r^2\\sin\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\left[\\frac{r^3}3\\right]_1^2[-\\cos\\theta]_{\\pi/3}^{\\pi/2}=\\frac73\\cdot\\frac12=\\frac76.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129632 :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=2x+y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/b5/b5ba69ab02aa13e201b7b9113f6a36e705ad19e6412499504afc4f239459a8f3.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129634 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v) \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v)$ and $y=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{2}{2u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{2u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{2u+v} & \\frac{1}{2u+v} \\\\ \\,\\text{ }v\\, & \\,\\text{ }u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2u+v}\\cdot u-\\frac{1}{2u+v}\\cdot v \\\\  & =\\frac{2u-v}{2u+v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129643 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle u = x - y, v = y,\n$$\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - y)^{2} - y = 0$ and the line $y = 1$." :question/worked-solution "Under $u=x-y$ and $v=y$, the parabola becomes $v=u^2$ and the line becomes $v=1$. They meet at $u=\\pm1$. The inverse is $x=u+v$, $y=v$, with Jacobian $1$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_{-1}^1\\int_{u^2}^1 1\\,dv\\,du\n=\\int_{-1}^1(1-u^2)\\,du=\\frac43.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129727 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +v\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=w.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $\\theta $ is a fixed constant." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129728 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{y}+z \\\\ {y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0$ and $y>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{y}+z,\\,z(x,y,z)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2y & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 2y & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}} & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{w} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129739 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ between the surfaces $z=1$ and $z=10-x.$\nExpress the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10-x=10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le z\\le 10-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}z\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}z\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3}}}{\\int }_{1}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}10-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}z}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129750 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4v \\\\ 3{u}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}(vw) \\\\ vw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=4v,\\,y(u,v,w)=3{u}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw),\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2w\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 6u & -2w\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) & -2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}6u & -2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ 0 & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4(6uv-0) \\\\  & =-24uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129751 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=-x+3y-2z,\\,w=3y+4z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(12+6)-1(-4-0) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129754 :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}+v \\\\ {e}^{w}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-v,\\,y(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+v,\\,z(u,v,w)={e}^{w}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{w}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{w}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -1 \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(2u+2u) \\\\  & =4u{e}^{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 4u{e}^{w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129767 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$  for $y\\ge 0$ and between the surfaces $z=-x$ and $z=2+x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/06/063fafc4f8c50dba56bcfb50b8366a2ffb8c713f5668ce22d10c0370376bbaba.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-x=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2+x=2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le 2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2z\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }2rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r[{z}^{2}]_{z=-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{z=2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r((2+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r+4{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{[2{r}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2(3)^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}(3)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }18+36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[18\\theta +36\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi } \\\\  & =18(\\pi -0)+36(0-0) \\\\  & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129768 :question/problem "What is the minimum value of $f(x) =-\\cos (x + \\frac{π}{4}) + 3$?" :question/worked-solution "As $\\cos(x+\\pi/4)$ ranges from $-1$ to $1$, its negative also ranges from $-1$ to $1$. Adding $3$ gives the range $[2,4]$. The minimum is $2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129780 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x) = 5\\cos x$?" :question/worked-solution "For $A\\cos x$, the amplitude is $|A|$. Here it is $|5|=5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129781 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x) = \\frac{3 - 2\\cos x}{13}$?" :question/worked-solution "Rewrite the function as $f(x)=3/13-(2/13)\\cos x$. Its amplitude is the absolute value of the cosine coefficient, $2/13$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129782 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period $T$ is given by $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129817 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y = \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (\\frac{1}{3}x)$." :question/worked-solution "For $A\\cos(Bx)$, the period is $2\\pi/|B|$. Here $B=1/3$, so the period is $6\\pi$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129878 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x+y)^{4}-x+y=0$ and  the line $y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $x-y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{4}-x+y=0\\,⟹\\,(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=u \\\\ x-y &=v\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{u-v}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the equation of the given line yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{u-v}{2}=0\\,⟹\\,v=u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{4} \\\\ v &=u\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}(u-1) &=0 \\\\ v &=u.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{4}\\le v\\le u\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/1e/1ed905e319e689d3fc7955a30f58e0f3ae2e7a751c27b80201d2b43f58b4206c.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(-2)^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{4}}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[v]_{{u}^{4}}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(u-{u}^{4})du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129887 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({sin}^{5}⁡0-{sin}^{5}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133486 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = y \\,\\mathbf{i} -x \\,\\mathbf{j} + z\\,\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\sin (\\pi t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos (\\pi t)\\, \\mathbf{j} + t(2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\sin (\\pi t), \\qquad\ny = \\cos (\\pi t), \\qquad\nz = t(2-t) = 2t-t^2.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2t-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2(1-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2t-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\hspace{0.167em})\\cdot (\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2(1-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t))+(2t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2(1-t) \\\\  & =\\pi {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(\\pi t)+\\pi {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(\\pi t)+2(2t-{t}^{2})(1-t) \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(\\pi t)+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}(\\pi t))+2(2t-3{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi +4t-6{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\pi +4t-6{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\pi t+2{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[2\\pi +8-16+8]-[0] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133487 :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\langle -xz, \\: -yz, \\: x+z^2 \\rangle,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz = g(x,y) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-xz,\\hspace{0.222em}-yz,\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}+x⟩{|}_{z=1} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-x}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{1+x}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 0-(-y)\\cdot 0+1+x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNow, note that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r \\sin\\theta, \\qquad\n\\textrm{d}A = r \\, \\textrm{d}r \\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[r\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133488 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t) = (t^2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (t^2-t) \\, \\mathbf{j} + (t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{i} + x\\, \\mathbf{j} + y\\,\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^2-t^3, \\qquad\ny = t^2-t, \\qquad\nz = t^2-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2t-3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ((2t-3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1)+({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}+(-{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}+t \\\\  & =3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133489 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z )\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k},$ express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =1+z+0 \\\\  & =z+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,z+1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133490 :question/problem "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\  & =2+1+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133491 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133642 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz}$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,\\sqrt 2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t) =t^2\\, \\mathbf{i} + t \\,\\mathbf{j} + 1 \\,\\mathbf{k},$ then along the curve we have $x = t^2,$ $y = t,$ and $z=1.$  \n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{yz},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t\\cdot 1}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u= 4t^2+1,$ $\\textrm d u=8t\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[{9}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[27-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 26 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133643 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$ given that $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y \\,\\mathbf i + (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf r(t) = (1+2t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (1-t)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0, 1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int_a^b\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r (t))\\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = 1 + 2t, \\qquad y = 1 - t .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(1-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+((1+2t)+(1-t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(1-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =((1-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(1-t)\\cdot 2+(t+2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2-2t-t-2 \\\\  & =-3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133644 :question/problem "Find an integral that is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf r(t)= \\left \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f5/f53d78debfec5f8c50e0f3a5947d2a36126e18c073d7b0cc4c59d506753059b3.png)\n\nThe path of integration is shown above. We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $f(x,y) =  xy^3$. Along the path, we have $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t,$ and so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =f(\\operatorname{cos}t,\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}t)^{3} \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int^{\\pi/2}_{0} \\sin^3 t \\cos t \\, \\textrm{d}t .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133645 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C}xy\\,\\textrm{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j}\\,$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j},$ then along the curve we have $x=3t$ and $y=4t.$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) =xy,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =3t\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12{t}^{2}\\cdot 5\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =60{[\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =20{[{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133646 :question/problem "Find a definite integral that's equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$ given that $\\mathbf F(x, y) = xy \\,\\mathbf i - x^2\\,\\mathbf j$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf r(t) = t^3\\,\\mathbf{i} + t^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0, 1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int_a^b\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r (t))\\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = t^3, \\qquad y = t^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =({t}^{3})({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-({t}^{3})^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{6}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =({t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{6}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{7}-2{t}^{7} \\\\  & ={t}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{7}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133692 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{3y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+0}+3(1)^{2}{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
